ds` containing all node ids initially (since we haven't removed any).\n6. Build a mapping from symbol to count of nodes with that symbol (among all nodes). But because we remove nodes, we need to update counts as we remove. Better: for each candidate removal, we check condition by simulating: for each required symbol, if the node's symbol equals that symbol and the count of nodes with that symbol in the current retained set (excluding this node) is zero, then removal would remove the last node of that required symbol, so not allowed. So we need to maintain a symbol count of retained nodes. Initialize with all nodes. When we remove a node, decrement its symbol count. For the tentative check, if node's symbol is in required, and after removal count becomes 0 (i.e., current count is 1), then fail. If node's symbol not required, no issue.\n7. Sort nodes by depth descending, and then by id ascending (for tie-break). Get list of node ids in that order.\n8. For each node id in that order:\n   - node = nodes_dict[id]\n   - Check conditions:\n     - node['removable'] must be True.\n     - node['depth'] != 0 (or id != 'root'? but root is depth 0, so depth>0)\n     - node['symbol'] not in required_symbols.\n     - No retained children: get children list from parent_map[parent] (if node id is parent). For each child in children, if child id is in retained_ids (i.e., not removed), then condition fails. (Since we process deeper first, children are already decided. If a child is retained, parent cannot be removed.)\n     - Symbol count condition: if node['symbol'] in required_symbols and current retained count of that symbol is 1 (i.e., this node is the last), then fail. (If symbol not required, always ok.)\n   - If all conditions true:\n     - Remove node: add node id to removed_ids, remove from retained_ids.\n     - Decrement symbol count for node['symbol'].\n9. After loop, retained_ids set contains ids of all nodes that were never removed. retained_node_ids should be sorted. removed_node_ids should be in the order we removed (the order we added to removed_ids list). Since we process nodes in the defined order and remove when possible, the list order is the removal order.\n10. Return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed_node_ids, \"retained_node_ids\": sorted(retained_ids)}.\n\nBut we need to check if the removal order is as expected: the output contract says removed_node_ids is array of strings, presumably in order of removal. So we should maintain a list in removal order.\n\nLet's test with the first fixture payload:\n\nnodes:\n- root (depth0, symbol root, removable false)\n- n1 (depth1, parent root, symbol s1, removable false) – required symbol s1, cannot remove because symbol in required, also removable false.\n- n2 (depth1, parent root, symbol s2, removable true)\n- n3 (depth1, parent root, symbol s0, removable false)\n- n4 (depth2, parent n2, symbol s1, removable true) – symbol s1 is required, so can't remove? removable true but required symbol? Condition says never remove a required symbol. So n4 cannot be removed.\n- n5 (depth2, parent n3, symbol s2, removable false)\n- n6 (depth2, parent n1, symbol s0, removable true)\n\nNow sort by depth descending: depth2 nodes: n4, n5, n6. Among them, tie-break by id: n4 < n5? Actually id strings: \"n4\", \"n5\", \"n6\" -> alphabetical: n4, n5, n6. So order: n4, n5, n6, then depth1: n1, n2, n3 (alphabetical: n1, n2, n3), then root.\n\nProcess n4: removable true, symbol s1 in required, cannot remove (condition: symbol in required). So skip.\nn5: removable false -> skip.\nn6: removable true, symbol s0 not required, depth>0, no children (depth2 leaf). Check retained children: no children. Symbol count condition: s0 count initial? Nodes with symbol s0: root has symbol root, n3 has s0, n6 has s0 = 2 nodes. After removal of n6, still have n3 with s0, so ok. So remove n6. removed_ids = [\"n6\"], retained_ids remove n6, symbol s0 count becomes 1.\nNow depth1: n1: symbol s1 required, not removable (removable false) -> skip.\nn2: removable true, symbol s2 not required, depth>0. Children: n4 is child (parent n2). n4 is still retained (not removed). So n2 has retained child -> cannot remove. So skip.\nn3: removable false, skip.\nroot: depth0, skip.\nAfter loop, retained_ids = all except n6. So retained_node_ids = [\"n1\",\"n2\",\"n3\",\"n4\",\"n5\",\"root\"] sorted: [\"n1\",\"n2\",\"n3\",\"n4\",\"n5\",\"root\"] (alphabetical). removed_node_ids = [\"n6\"].\n\nIs that correct? Expected? We don't have ground truth, but seems plausible.\n\nBut note: The problem statement mentions \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\" and \"miniature grammar\". But we are not given grammar. The only info is nodes have symbols and parent relationships. The required_symbols list likely corresponds to nonterminals that must be present. So removal preserves at least one node with each required symbol. That's what we did.\n\nPotential nuance: \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" We interpreted as children that are in retained set. But what if a node has children that are removed? Then it's okay. That's what we did.\n\nAlso, we considered removable flag. But what about nodes with removable false? They cannot be removed regardless. That's correct.\n\nNow we need to implement solve(case) in Python, returning a JSON-serializable dict. Use standard library only. No imports beyond maybe json, but we can just return dict.\n\nEdge cases: required_symbols may include root, so root cannot be removed. Also, if a required symbol appears multiple times, we can remove all but one. But we only remove nodes if they are removable and not required-symbol, and if removing doesn't eliminate the last occurrence.\n\nNow implement.\n\nWe must ensure we handle the case where some nodes have no parent (root has parent null). In parent mapping: for root, parent is null, so no parent entry. For children mapping, we build from node's parent. So for each node, if parent is not None, we add node id to children_of[parent].\n\nOne more thing: the condition \"deeper before shallower\" is by depth descending. But nodes at same depth: we said tie-break by id ascending. The problem says \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" So yes.\n\nNow produce final answer.\n\nMake sure output is exactly {\"removed_node_ids\": list of strings in removal order, \"retained_node_ids\": sorted list of strings}. Sorting retained: use sorted().\n\nNow we write the code.\n\nBut also note: the output schema says \"dynamicKeysFrom means the object keys are arbitrary JSON strings that must be copied verbatim from the indicated input location; never parse, coerce, numerically validate, or impose a naming pattern on those keys.\" But that's for other tasks, not relevant here.\n\nOur output keys are fixed: removed_node_ids, retained_node_ids.\n\nSo code:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    required_symbols = case['required_symbols']\n    # Build dict\n    node_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\n    # Build children mapping\n    children_of = {}\n    for n in nodes:\n        pid = n['parent']\n        if pid is not None:\n            children_of.setdefault(pid, []).append(n['id'])\n    # Initialize sets\n    all_ids = set(n['id'] for n in nodes)\n    retained = set(all_ids)\n    removed = []  # list in order\n    # Symbol counts\n    sym_count = {}\n    for n in nodes:\n        sym = n['symbol']\n        sym_count[sym] = sym_count.get(sym, 0) + 1\n    # Sort nodes by depth descending, then id ascending\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\n        nid = node['id']\n        # Check if already removed? Not necessary because we only process each once.\n        # Conditions\n        if not node['removable']:\n            continue\n        # Not root\n        if node['depth'] == 0:\n            continue\n        # Not required symbol\n        if node['symbol'] in required_symbols:\n            continue\n        # No retained children\n        children = children_of.get(nid, [])\n        if any(cid in retained for cid in children):\n            continue\n        # Check symbol count condition\n        sym = node['symbol']\n        if sym in required_symbols:\n            # after removal, count must be >0\n            if sym_count.get(sym, 0) <= 1:\n                continue\n        # All conditions satisfied: remove\n        removed.append(nid)\n        retained.remove(nid)\n        sym_count[sym] = sym_count.get(sym, 0) - 1\n    # Sort retained\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\n    return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed, \"retained_node_ids\": retained_sorted}\n\nBut careful: children_of mapping should include nodes even if they have no children? Not needed. Also, for leaf nodes, children_of.get returns empty list, fine.\n\nAlso, note: we need to handle the case where a node's parent is null (root). In children_of, for root, we have entries from children that have parent 'root'. So that's fine.\n\nNow final response as JSON with key \"implementation\" containing the source string.\n\nWe must escape the Python code as JSON string: newlines become \\n, backslashes become \\\\, quotes become \\\". We'll write the code inside triple quotes but then need to escape. Since the code is short, we can do manually or use Python's json.dumps on the code string. In the response, we will just write the escaped string.\n\nBetter to provide the exact JSON structure with escaped code.\n\nWe'll write the final answer.\n\nMake sure no extra text, just JSON."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2085,"completion_tokens":4831,"total_tokens":6916,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":4464},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2048,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":37},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}